WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:01.920
INTRO:
The S&amp;P 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.760
is often hailed as the crown jewel of personal&nbsp;
finance. If you want to reliably grow your money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.760
and you don’t want to go through earnings&nbsp;
reports or experience extreme volatility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.440
the S&amp;P 500 is usually the top recommendation.&nbsp;
Everyone from YouTubers like Graham Stephan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.760
deca billionaires like Warren Buffett are massive&nbsp;
fans of the S&amp;P 500, and this is for good reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.120
The S&amp;P 500 not only gives you a chance to&nbsp;
be invested in the leading American companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.160
but also an opportunity to diversify amongst&nbsp;
hundreds of companies. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.920
like Warren Buffett says, investing in the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 is basically like betting on America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.360
And for the past few hundred years, that’s&nbsp;
been an extremely successful bet. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.320
a few notable investors like Michael Burry have&nbsp;
actually been warning us that index funds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.840
passive investing in general is one of the biggest&nbsp;
bubbles of our time. Now, this probably doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.800
mean that you should sell everything and go&nbsp;
into cash, but it’s also likely a good idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.760
to look at why exactly Michael Burry believes&nbsp;
this. So, here’s the index fund bubble theory.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.880
SHORT HISTORY:&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.400
One of the biggest factors people cite in&nbsp;
favor of the S&amp;P 500 is its long history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:23.920
You hear things like, the S&amp;P 500 has returned&nbsp;
an average of 10.5% for the past 100 years. Such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.760
statements make you think that the S&amp;P 500 has&nbsp;
been a staple investment for basically a century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.280
and that it has performed strongly even through&nbsp;
events such as World War 2 and the cold war, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.920
this isn’t exactly true. While the S&amp;P 500 and the&nbsp;
Dow Jones have been around since the early 1900s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.120
they were never popular investment choices.&nbsp;
In fact, there was no easy way to even invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:53.840
in the S&amp;P 500. If you were a retail investor,&nbsp;
you had go out and buy 500 different stocks. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.520
wasn’t until 1976 that John bogle, the founder&nbsp;
of Vanguard, launched the first US index fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.040
that was available to retail investors. This&nbsp;
means that for the majority of the 1900s the S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.360
500 was simply an indicator like the RSI or the&nbsp;
MACD as opposed to an actual investment. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.600
it’s not like the S&amp;P 500 just took off as soon as&nbsp;
John Bogle launched the fund. In fact, interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:24.160
the fund was horrendous. John was hoping that&nbsp;
$150 million would flow into the fund at IPO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.680
but in reality, only $11.3 million flowed&nbsp;
in. Not only did the fund perform poorly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.640
a financial perspective, but it was often&nbsp;
the laughing stock of Wall Street. The S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.720
500 was generally associated with unsophisticated&nbsp;
investors who couldn’t even beat the market. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.480
it should be noted that active funds themselves&nbsp;
were often not beating the market, but this didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.080
matter. The vast majority of people bought into&nbsp;
the narrative that active funds were superior and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.160
that only losers who didn’t have access to active&nbsp;
funds invested in something like the S&amp;P 500. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.840
was the overwhelming sentiment up until the early&nbsp;
2000s when we saw the dotcom crash. A lot of tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.720
heavy funds got obliterated or completely wiped&nbsp;
out while the S&amp;P 500 held up decently well. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.960
it should be noted that the S&amp;P 500 itself was&nbsp;
down about 50%, but this was far better than a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:23.520
of internet companies that were down 80,90,95%.&nbsp;
This led to more interest in index funds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.960
consequently, more index funds and ETFS were&nbsp;
launched. But, it wasn’t until the 2008 financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.160
recession and Warren Buffett’s famous bet against&nbsp;
active funds that index funds really took off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.680
Just take a look at this graph showcasing&nbsp;
the assets under management by ETFS. In 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.840
this number was just $200 billion worldwide.&nbsp;
Today, this number stands at over $10 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.840
and much of this growth has happened within the&nbsp;
past decade. So clearly, index fund investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.440
is not some tried and true strategy that has been&nbsp;
around for a 100 years. Really, it wasn’t till the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.800
last two decades that index fund investing become&nbsp;
a popular strategy. And this short history opens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.240
up index fund investing to a massive slew&nbsp;
of problems starting with asset inflation.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.120
ASSET INFLATION:&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.880
While active investing often gets a lot of&nbsp;
slack nowadays for not even beating the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.520
when you really think about it, it’s actually&nbsp;
active investing that made index funds so great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.000
in the first place. The reason that index funds&nbsp;
were able to post such great numbers throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.120
the 1900s was because index funds were basically&nbsp;
a conglomeration of active investors favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.720
stocks. For example, if a lot of active investors&nbsp;
really liked Coca-Cola, the stock would go up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.320
and the market cap would go up, and it would be&nbsp;
included in the S&amp;P 500. Conversely, if a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.120
active investors didn’t like Coca-Cola, the stock&nbsp;
would go down and the market cap would go down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.480
and it would be kicked from the S&amp;P 500. So, it&nbsp;
was really the active investors that indirectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.440
determined which stocks would be in the index, and&nbsp;
the index was basically a consensus of the active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.120
investors. With the rise of passive investing,&nbsp;
however, this is no longer the case. Many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.400
companies in the S&amp;P 500 receive massive amounts&nbsp;
of buying pressure simply because they’re part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.520
of the index. For example, have you guys ever&nbsp;
heard of Nielsen Holdings, Zions Bancorporation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.280
BorgWarner, or Pinnacle West Captial. I doubt&nbsp;
any of these stocks have massive active investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.920
bases. However, they boast quite a bit of volume.&nbsp;
BorgWarner boasts an average daily volume of 2.16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:31.280
million shares and Nielsen holdings boasts an&nbsp;
average daily volume of 10.72 million shares.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.360
This usually correlates to tens of millions of&nbsp;
dollars if not hundreds of millions of dollars on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.960
a daily basis. And much of this volume is buying&nbsp;
pressure from passive investors who simply invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.480
a set amount every day or week. These guys aren’t&nbsp;
looking at the company’s PE ratios or its revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.200
growth or its profit growth or any of the factors&nbsp;
that determine whether the given company is a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.480
investment. Despite this, they continue to buy in&nbsp;
on a regular basis just because it’s part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.240
index. I don’t think I need to explain how this&nbsp;
leads to these stocks being inflated. Overtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.480
this leads to bad companies sticking around&nbsp;
in the index for much longer than they should,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.600
and it prevents great companies from entering the&nbsp;
index when they should. This concern also carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:17.440
over into the biggest companies in the index&nbsp;
as well. For 9 in 10 companies in the S&amp;P 500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.320
their largest single shareholder is an index&nbsp;
fund provider. This means that stocks like Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.000
Google, Amazon, and Facebook receive extraordinary&nbsp;
amounts of buying pressure from passive investors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.480
Now, there’s no doubt that these are&nbsp;
phenomenal companies, but it’s likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.760
even these companies would be valued much lower&nbsp;
if they weren’t part of the index. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.320
Apple might be worth $2 trillion instead of&nbsp;
$3 trillion if it wasn’t for constant buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.440
pressure from passive investors. This eventually&nbsp;
leads to a much bigger problem than simply asset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.200
inflation. If passive investing gets big enough,&nbsp;
which it might already be, index funds will start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.920
performing well not because the companies in the&nbsp;
index are fundamentally performing well but simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.400
because of supply and demand, and that’s a&nbsp;
rabbit hole that we don’t want to go into.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.480
NOT DIVERSIFIED:&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:13.120
Another major shortfall of the S&amp;P 500 is that&nbsp;
it’s not actually that diversified. But wait a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:19.360
minute, 500 companies is a lot of companies.&nbsp;
If we split $100 dollars into 500 companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:24.560
each company would only get a 20 cents.&nbsp;
This means that you’re only risking 0.2%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:30.000
of your investment with any single company&nbsp;
right? Well, this would be true if the S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.280
500 was an equal weight index but it’s not.&nbsp;
Apple alone accounts for nearly 7% of the index,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.240
and the top 50 companies account for over&nbsp;
50% of the index. This means that the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.840
450 companies don’t even account for half of&nbsp;
your investment. This lack of diversification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:53.280
significantly accentuates the asset inflation&nbsp;
problem. Also, not only do these top companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.280
account for the majority of the S&amp;P 500 in&nbsp;
terms of weight, but they also account for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:03.760
the majority of its returns. In fact, just 5&nbsp;
companies, Facebook, Amazon, Apple, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:10.560
and Google have accounted for 40% of the S&amp;P 500’s&nbsp;
total returns over the past 5 years. Another way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:17.200
to put this is that 1% of the S&amp;P 500 accounted&nbsp;
for 40% of its success. If we just remove these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.480
5 companies from the index, the S&amp;P 500’s&nbsp;
annual average return would drop from 9.68%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:29.200
down to 5.76%. Now, this is already quite&nbsp;
substantial, but the contribution becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.720
even bigger if we consider the top 100 companies.&nbsp;
To make this comparison, let’s take a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:41.360
S&amp;P 100 which is exactly what it sounds like.&nbsp;
It’s the top 100 companies in the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.680
In this graph, the S&amp;P 500 is the orange line&nbsp;
and the S&amp;P 100 is the candles. As we can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:54.240
sometimes the S&amp;P 500 is winning and other times&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 100 is winning, but over the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:01.600
they’re basically identical. Over the past 32&nbsp;
years, the S&amp;P 500 has only beaten out the S&amp;P 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:09.120
by a mere 20%. This means that 98.3% of the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 returns over the past 32 years are from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.200
top 100 companies in the S&amp;P 500. Where things get&nbsp;
really interesting though is when we compare both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:20.720
of these to the Nasdaq 100. If you’re not familiar&nbsp;
with what the Nasdaq 100 is, it’s basically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:27.280
top 100 tech companies in the S&amp;P 500. And if we&nbsp;
add the nasdaq to our graph, this is what we get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:32.720
As you can see, the Nasdaq literally performed&nbsp;
5 times better than the S&amp;P 500 over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.400
32 years. And given that the S&amp;P basically&nbsp;
includes all the stocks that are in the Nasdaq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:44.320
this means that the other 400 companies in the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 straight up holding back performance. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.520
makes perfect sense when you consider that the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 includes stocks like AT&amp;T, Citigroup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.640
American Airlines, and General Motors which&nbsp;
are down on a multi decade basis. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:59.200
such companies account for a smaller weight&nbsp;
of the S&amp;P 500 than the top performers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.120
but that doesn’t change that billions of&nbsp;
dollars are flowing into such companies not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.480
because they’re top performers, but just because&nbsp;
they’re part of the S&amp;P 500. And as more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.360
start investing in indices grow their positions,&nbsp;
this just becomes a bigger and bigger problem.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.440
TRUTH ABOUT INDEX FUNDS:&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.840
At the end of the day, index funds may not&nbsp;
actually be the safe haven that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.280
often pitched as. Index funds have a relatively&nbsp;
short history of actually being invested into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.920
and the rise of passive investing is likely&nbsp;
leading to significant asset inflation. Not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:37.760
mention, you can get far better returns by simply&nbsp;
investing into the top performers of the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:43.253
This is why many top investors aren’t big fans of&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500 or are straight up calling for a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.253 --> 00:10:49.200
on collapse like Michael Burry. With that being&nbsp;
said, should you stop investing in the S&amp;P 500?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.200
Well, this is not financial advice and I’m just&nbsp;
some guy on the internet, but here’s what I think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:00.480
While the S&amp;P 500 does have the risk of being&nbsp;
the centerpiece of a massive bubble, I strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.680
believe that the risk of not investing is way&nbsp;
higher than the risk of investing in the S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.600
500. The only thing that is guaranteed to go down&nbsp;
in real value is cash, and this is more relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.800
than ever before given the sky high inflation.&nbsp;
And currently, one of the best ways we know to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.080
mitigate this risk is to invest in the S&amp;P 500&nbsp;
which has thus far produced moderate yet reliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.200
returns. Now, if you’re willing to stomach some&nbsp;
more volatility, I think the Nasdaq 100 may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.160
an even better choice. And if you’re willing to&nbsp;
take even more risk you might consider investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.840
in just the top 20 or 30 tech companies along&nbsp;
with some bitcoin and ethereum. But, as a base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:43.600
I think everyone should atleast have some exposure&nbsp;
to the S&amp;P 500. That way, your money is not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.720
burning away in some low interest savings account.&nbsp;
But that’s just what I think. Do you guys think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:54.320
that the S&amp;P 500 is just a massive bubble? Comment&nbsp;
that down below. Also, drop a like if you’re a fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:58.720
of the S&amp;P 500. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
out our international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.640
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
